  "holistic_F_N": 1,
  "holistic_F_H": 0,
  "section_notes": "S1 is actually stricter than the safe baseline: every thermal incident (including those still under investigation) must reach the regulator within 24 hours and affected customers within 72 hours, which hurts both firms (NW 3 and HC 4 unreported incidents). S2 keeps 55 °C / 5% and gives numeric per-model results to purchasers and regulators without public publication, but the 24-month corrective-plan/retest window with no automatic sales ban and only discretionary regulator sales restrictions softens the consequence for Northwind's Hearth 10. S3 satisfies NW-2 with a 6-year retrofit and immediate coverage of new units, but the bare 'where technically feasible' escape (only annual progress reporting, no alternative safety measure) leaves the retrofit obligation open-ended; S4 lands exactly on Halcyon's 36-month red line (built-in required, within the ≤48-month acceptable band), and S5 keeps the mandatory 8/100k numeric recall trigger but lets the regulator decide whether distribution stops and confirms a recall does not by itself permanently bar sales."
 },
